17592186433575 1079 #uuid "d50a82b7-c1b2-5c98-ba51-6c3723f45143" 13194139534710 17592186045417 true] [17592186433576 1075 #edb/ref 17592186433568 13194139534710 17592186045417 true] [17592186433576 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139534710 17592186045417 true] [17592186433576 1077 "field-2" 13194139534710 17592186045417 true] [17592186433576 1078 #edb/ref 17592186433568 13194139534710 17592186045417 true] [17592186433576 1079 #uuid "e9385044-6a5a-538b-bbb6-bb685a6c0891" 13194139534710 17592186045417 true] [17592186433577 1075 #edb/ref 17592186433563 13194139534710 17592186045417 true] [17592186433577 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139534710 17592186045417 true] [17592186433577 1077 "field-1" 13194139534710 17592186045417 true] [17592186433577 1078 #edb/ref 17592186433563 13194139534710 17592186045417 true] [17592186433577 1079 #uuid "ef06f9a9-3a61-51eb-ac5d-98d58a355d86" 13194139534710 17592186045417 true] [17592186433578 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433573 13194139534710 17592186045417 true] [17592186433578 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433574 13194139534710 17592186045417 true] [17592186433578 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433576 13194139534710 17592186045417 true] [17592186433578 1069 "q-333228" 13194139534710 17592186045417 true] [17592186433578 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534710 17592186045417 true] [17592186433578 1071 "An initial value problem has the exact solution ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)={e}^{0.3x}.$ A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to the initial value problem, resulting in the table below, where $y$ denotes the approximate solution at each step, and ${τ}_{n}$ is the local truncation error at each step. At each step, the exact value is used to approximate the new value. \n\nCalculate the local truncation error at each step. Round your answers to three decimal places.\n\n| $n$ | $x$ | $y$ | ${τ}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.000$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.19$ | $1.052$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $2$ | $0.38$ | $1.123$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $3$ | $0.57$ | $1.181$ | {{field-3}} |" 13194139534710 17592186045417 true] [17592186433578 1072 #uuid "0a3924b0-b0f6-5c7e-a004-258ec4f48a4c" 13194139534710 17592186045417 true] [17592186433578 1074 "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a *single step* of a numerical method, when the step begins from an *exact value*. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the LTE at each step, rounding to three decimal places in each case:\n\n- After the first step, we have ${x}_{\\text{new}}=0.19$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}=1.052.$ So, the LTE of the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{1} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.19)-1.052 \\\\  & ={e}^{0.3\\cdot 0.19}-1.052 \\\\  & ≈0.007.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- After the second step, we have ${x}_{\\text{new}}=0.38$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}=1.123.$ So, the LTE of the second step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{2} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.38)-1.123 \\\\  & ={e}^{0.3\\cdot 0.38}-1.123 \\\\  & ≈-0.002.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- After the third step, we have ${x}_{\\text{new}}=0.57$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}=1.181.$ So, the LTE of the third step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{3} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.57)-1.181 \\\\  & ={e}^{0.3\\cdot 0.57}-1.181 \\\\  & ≈0.005.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $n$ | $x$ | $y$ | ${τ}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.000$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.19$ | $1.052$ | $0.007$ |\n| $2$ | $0.38$ | $1.123$ | $-0.002$ |\n| $3$ | $0.57$ | $1.181$ | $0.005$ |" 13194139534710 17592186045417 true] [17592186433579 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433571 13194139534710 17592186045417 true] [17592186433579 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433575 13194139534710 17592186045417 true] [17592186433579 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433577 13194139534710 17592186045417 true] [17592186433579 1069 "q-333165" 13194139534710 17592186045417 true] [17592186433579 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534710 17592186045417 true] [17592186433579 1071 "An initial value problem has the exact solution ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to the initial value problem, resulting in the table below, where $y$ denotes the approximate solution at each step, and ${τ}_{n}$ is the local truncation error at each step. At each step, the exact value is used to approximate the new value. \n\nCalculate the local truncation error at each step. Round your answers to three decimal places.\n\n| $n$ | $x$ | $y$ | ${τ}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.000$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.25$ | $0.970$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $2$ | $0.5$ | $0.880$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $3$ | $0.75$ | $0.735$ | {{field-3}} |" 13194139534710 17592186045417 true] [17592186433579 1072 #uuid "3eda7141-5dd2-5618-9d66-39b067ed39a7" 13194139534710 17592186045417 true] [17592186433579 1074 "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a *single step* of a numerical method, when the step begins from an *exact value*. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the LTE at each step, rounding to three decimal places in each case:\n\n- After the first step, we have ${y}_{\\text{new}}=0.970.$ So, the LTE of the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{1} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.25)-0.970 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.25)-0.970 \\\\  & ≈-0.001.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- After the second step, we have ${y}_{\\text{new}}=0.880.$ So, the LTE of the second step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{2} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.5)-0.880 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.5)-0.880 \\\\  & ≈-0.002.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- After the third step, we have ${y}_{\\text{new}}=0.735.$ So, the LTE of the third step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{3} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.75)-0.735 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.75)-0.735 \\\\  & ≈-0.003.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $n$ | $x$ | $y$ | ${τ}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.000$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.25$ | $0.970$ | $-0.001$ |\n| $2$ | $0.5$ | $0.880$ | $-0.002$ |\n| $3$ | $0.75$ | $0.735$ | $-0.003$ |" 13194139534710 17592186045417 true] [17592186433580 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433570 13194139534710 17592186045417 true] [17592186433580 1069 "q-332884" 13194139534710 17592186045417 true] [17592186433580 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534710 17592186045417 true] [17592186433580 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nSuppose we wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the exact solution is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)={e}^{2x},$ compute the local truncation error ${τ}_{11}$ for the step starting at $x=1$ with step size $Δx=0.1.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${τ}_{11}≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534710 17592186045417 true] [17592186433580 1072 #uuid "7ac21dac-d2df-5087-933a-d3cb9d951740" 13194139534710 17592186045417 true] [17592186433580 1074 "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a *single step* of a numerical method, when the step begins from an *exact value*. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact solution is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)={e}^{2x}.$ So, the $y$-value at the start of the step $x=1$ is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(1)={e}^{2}.$\n\nNow, let's find the $y$-increment given by the numerical method.\n\nFirst, the slope at the start of the step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(1) & =2{y}_{\\text{exact}}(1) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {e}^{2} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the increment at this step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(1)\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2{e}^{2}\\cdot 0.1 \\\\  & =0.2{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at ${x}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(x+Δx) \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(1+0.1) \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(1.1) \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\cdot 1.1} \\\\  & ={e}^{2.2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for the step starting at $x=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{11} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(1.1)-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(1)+Δy) \\\\  & ={e}^{2.2}-({e}^{2}+0.2{e}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2.2}-1.2{e}^{2} \\\\  & ≈0.158\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534710 17592186045417 true] [17592186433581 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433572 13194139534710 17592186045417 true] [17592186433581 1069 "q-334177" 13194139534710 17592186045417 true] [17592186433581 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534710 17592186045417 true] [17592186433581 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x+4,\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nSuppose we wish to approximate the solution using the following numerical method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{2}+{y}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the exact solution is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)={x}^{2}+4x,$ compute the local truncation error, ${τ}_{6},$ for the step starting at $x=1$ with step size $Δx=0.2.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${τ}_{6}≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534710 17592186045417 true] [17592186433581 1072 #uuid "8d3b00c2-9b00-52d7-ad0c-fe79f07b8ec3" 13194139534710 17592186045417 true] [17592186433581 1074 "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a *single step* of a numerical method, when the step begins from an *exact value*. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact solution is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)={x}^{2}+4x.$ So, the $y$-value at the start of the step $x=1$ is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(1)=5.$\n\nNow, let's find the $y$-increment given by the numerical method.\n\nFirst, the slope at the start of the step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(1) & =2(1)+4 \\\\  & =2+4 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the increment at this step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{2}+{y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(1))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(1+6)\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & ≈0.4667.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at ${x}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(x+Δx) \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(1+0.2) \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(1.2) \\\\  & =(1.2)^{2}+4(1.2) \\\\  & =6.24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for the step starting at $x=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{6} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(1.2)-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(1)+Δy) \\\\  & =6.24-(5+0.4667) \\\\  & ≈0.773\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534710 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-070795fc-61ca-597e-9a52-6ea22d8e9b4d" 17592186433562 "answer-07626de1-5e6b-55e7-b945-409da489d200" 17592186433563 "answer-22719ae7-bcde-57eb-9c63-c4a0060ab2b6" 17592186433564 "answer-68ea8446-6ec7-5d53-833e-6da073d1e40c" 17592186433565 "answer-a9f9ab51-ea1d-532c-a7fa-adb5fd089752" 17592186433566 "answer-bfa58bf2-846f-52fd-9f0f-0ee0d002d866" 